WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.281 --> 00:00:01.694
- [Narrator] This is indigo.

00:00:01.694 --> 00:00:03.389
Real indigo.

00:00:03.389 --> 00:00:06.049
Jeans, they're dyed from a chemical.

00:00:06.049 --> 00:00:08.836
But real indigo straight
from the plant has its roots

00:00:08.836 --> 00:00:13.650
in Samurai culture and is
highly prized in Japan.

00:00:13.650 --> 00:00:16.756
Yep, this story is all
about the real color blue.

00:00:16.756 --> 00:00:19.589
But it's not as dull as it sounds.

00:00:23.660 --> 00:00:26.393
Tokushima, Japan is located in
the southwest of the country

00:00:26.393 --> 00:00:29.432
and for centuries, it has
been known for its indigo

00:00:29.432 --> 00:00:32.452
due to the Yoshino-gawa
River, which flows underground

00:00:32.452 --> 00:00:34.336
warming the soil.

00:00:34.336 --> 00:00:37.263
Indigo started gaining
popularity in the early 1600s

00:00:37.263 --> 00:00:40.096
and was commonly used by Samurais.

00:00:46.573 --> 00:00:48.075
This is Osamu Nii.

00:00:48.075 --> 00:00:51.742
He's a sixth generation
indigo plant grower.

00:01:03.417 --> 00:01:05.959
Real indigo was also used
for firefighters' clothing

00:01:05.959 --> 00:01:10.126
because it's flame retardant,
up to 1500 degrees Fahrenheit.

00:01:11.708 --> 00:01:14.109
To produce indigo ink, the
leaves must be harvested

00:01:14.109 --> 00:01:17.303
and then dried in the sun
and flipped repeatedly

00:01:17.303 --> 00:01:20.636
with a broom until they turn a navy hue.

00:01:25.365 --> 00:01:27.813
Because of this, Osamu
is one of the last five

00:01:27.813 --> 00:01:30.021
indigo growers in the region.

00:01:30.021 --> 00:01:33.520
The practice is time-consuming
and labor intensive,

00:01:33.520 --> 00:01:35.502
so former growers have turned to

00:01:35.502 --> 00:01:37.891
cheaper chemical alternatives.

00:01:37.891 --> 00:01:39.494
But the thing is, with real indigo,

00:01:39.494 --> 00:01:42.965
the color you can achieve is unparalleled.

00:01:42.965 --> 00:01:45.008
This is Toshiharu Furusho.

00:01:45.008 --> 00:01:46.591
He's a fabric dyer.

00:01:56.638 --> 00:01:59.028
He's able to pull out an
incredible range of blues

00:01:59.028 --> 00:02:00.661
from the natural ink.

00:02:00.661 --> 00:02:03.323
From a nearly black
blue they call nando-iro

00:02:03.323 --> 00:02:06.382
to deep ocean hues called kachi-iro.

00:02:06.382 --> 00:02:09.882
Achieving these colors though is not easy.

00:02:17.334 --> 00:02:19.579
His commitment to his craft
can be seen in his hands.

00:02:19.579 --> 00:02:22.340
His nails are eternally
stained blue from nearly

00:02:22.340 --> 00:02:25.090
50 years of working with the dye.

00:02:33.568 --> 00:02:37.735
And Osamu, he's not hanging
up his trowel yet either.

